{"type": "say", "text": "Ethan, I agree the forensic inspection and the transition carve-out are compatible — one goes to likelihood of success, the other to the scope of the remedy. I'm inclined to approve the approach with two conditions. First, the supplemental-evidence filing needs to go in within the next week, as you suggested, and it should include both the signed confidentiality agreement and the 4,800-file copy logs with a clear chain-of-custody declaration. Second, the forensic-inspection request needs to be narrowly tailored — we're asking the court for access to the competitor's systems to identify our client's confidential software, not a fishing expedition. Opposing counsel will fight this, so the proposed order for the inspection needs to be specific about scope, timing, and a neutral third-party vendor if possible. On the injunction itself, the proposed order will include the 30-day customer-transition window for existing hosted customers and will not require a shutdown of the customer-facing service during that period. After 30 days, the competitor must cease all use of the confidential software. If there are no other disclosures — evidence, constraints, or filing commitments — I'm prepared to approve this strategy and move to finalizing the motion papers."}
